Using the sliders, you can explore the dynamics of the system for different values of \(a\) and \(b\) and visualize the different types of equilibrium points listed in the table above. Although the axes are scaled from -1 to 3, this system is supposed to represent populations, which are strictly non-negative. Therefore, for our modeling purposes, we only consider the first quadrant. But to understand the full mathematical picture, I zoomed it out a bit ;) .
Move the sliders to change \(a\) and \(b\). Try to observe the types of fixed points you see in the table and the bifurcations that occur at the critical values!


